INT. CURLING RINK - 5 A.M.

Empty ice. Frost smoke on the boards. A zamboni hums somewhere far off.

INGRID SOLBERG, thirties, national-team jacket, sits on the hack with a polishing cloth. She buffs one granite stone. Slow circles. Calm.

She sets it down. Picks up another.

A door CLANKS at the far end. Footsteps on rubber matting. Ingrid doesn't look up.

KAMALA JOSHI, twenties, rookie, duffel bag, bright eyes. Too bright. She stops at the boards.

KAMALA
You're early.

INGRID
Always.

KAMALA
Coach said six.

INGRID
Coach says a lot.

Kamala steps onto the ice. Slides carefully. Settles on a bench near the house.

KAMALA
I watched your Oslo final. Eleven times.

INGRID
Then you know the ending.

KAMALA
She misses the draw. You win.

INGRID
(beat)
She doesn't miss. I did.

Kamala laughs. Ingrid doesn't. The cloth keeps circling.

KAMALA
I'm second skip. They told me last night.

Ingrid's hand stops. A breath. She sets the stone down gently.

INGRID
Did they say whose spot?

KAMALA
No. Just that it's open.

INGRID
It's not open.

She stands. Walks to the sheet. Her jacket reads CAPTAIN in small stitching.

INGRID (CONT'D)
I signed the release at midnight.

Kamala's face falls.

KAMALA
You quit?

INGRID
I was cut. She was told to keep it quiet until you'd arrived.

KAMALA
Who told you?

INGRID
Coach. Same one who said six.

She holds out the stone's handle to Kamala.

INGRID (CONT'D)
Throw it.

KAMALA
I don't--

INGRID
Throw it. I want to see what they chose.

Kamala takes the handle. Sets her feet. Pushes. The stone glides, long and clean, to the button.

Ingrid watches it stop. A small nod.

INGRID (CONT'D)
Good.

She picks up her bag and walks off the ice.
